A||N||11||NOS3800||Is NOS invisible?||||5||
Z||000000||NOS3800||07-15-2001||12:19 PM||||Question: Is straight NOS out of the bottle visible? <BR>I took my intake off so I can test the NOS nozzle( dry kit) cause it sounded like it was only firing intermittenetly down the track. Well, I loaded the line then turned the bottle off. I then opened the throttle to WOT and when the nozzle fired it definetely sounded like it fired but no visible gas. Also, it wasn't very cold. <BR>I have seen cars at the track purge their systems with a big plume of spray that is visible from the stands. Is it because I closed the bottle? My times sucked Friday nite and it was 60 degrees. I had half a bottle of NOS. Lost 4 tenths off my best time which was run on a 80degree nite. Any help?<P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||5
Z||000001||jake1852||07-15-2001||12:49 PM||||good 'ol hippie crack.<p>[This message has been edited by jake1852 (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Toddster||07-15-2001||12:50 PM||||NO2 does not have a "color" if that is what you mean. But it should make a mist upon exposure to the atmosphere.<P>NO2 is not explosive (common misconception) so don't worry about about purging it to test the system. NO2 just increases the amount of oxygen in the combustion chamber making the expolsive reaction more efficient.<P>If you are seeing nothing then you may have a stuck valve or leaky bottle.||||reg||1
Z||000003||jake1852||07-15-2001||12:50 PM||||here, this is what i know - <P>when you inhale nitrus, it is very cold, and you run the risk of freezing your lungs up. it is a clear gas, but if a huge shot gets let off you might see something that looks like fog somewhat. <P>but if its not cold, its not nitrus! (maybe someone sold you a bum bottle?)<P>------------------<BR>- Unfinished ferrari kit, custom made myself<BR>-87 duke (just got it, its fast, and i dont know why yet!)<BR>- 87 GT<BR>- Tc Maserati (daily drive)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||07-15-2001||01:33 PM||||Pure NO(N<sub>2</sub>O? I can't remember right now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ) is colorless and odourless. When released quickly, it condenses the moisture in the air, creating 'fog.' If it's released slowly, it heats up too fast, and will have no effect. NO behaves like any other compressed gas in this way. You may have a clogged bottle filter? If it hisses, but you don't see anything, it's coming out too slow. Bad valve on the bottle, or some other restriction. Oh, and I don't recommend inhaling automotive NO for recreational purposes. Not unless you want to see what you've eaten for the past couple of days. If you need a NO fix that bad, go buy a can of whipped cream, and suck on that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||grinthock||07-15-2001||01:34 PM||||I do'nt know much about NOS systems, but your nozzle should be screwed into the intake, take it out, point it away from you (or at a peice of cardboard or osmething, and watch as you press the microswitch, you should see a visible mist, NOS is colourless, but it's a liquid, so it will appear as a mist.  And because of the pressure coming out of the bottle if you spray it at a surface you should get kinda a condensation.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sluppy123||07-15-2001||03:01 PM||||Did you have the car running or have the ingition on when you did this test? if not, you probaly only heard the first solinoid opening, the second soliniod will only open if the right Fuel PSI is detected. You'll know it whrn it fires, it's very visible when its's sprayed out of the nozzle.<P>And I wouldn't recomend trying to inhale automotive grade N2O, It contains trace amounts of sulfer, bad sh*t to breath.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Eibach springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 70hp NOS, 17" Mille Miglia's, Turbo install in progress.<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroHarry||07-15-2001||09:21 PM||||See above post, the sulfur Nos has some color to it and can also cause you to smoke some depending on how much has been charged into the nitrous.......||||reg||1
Z||000008||NOS3800||07-16-2001||12:03 AM||||When I tested the nozzle the bottle valve was CLOSED. So I guess there wasn't enough pressure in the line to create the mist, or being that the ignition was "ON" but the car was not running the second solenoid did not open. <BR>Thanks for the help||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||07-16-2001||12:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Toddster:<BR><B>NO2 is not explosive (common misconception) so don't worry about about purging it to test the system. NO2 just increases the amount of oxygen in the combustion chamber making the expolsive reaction more efficient.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Um, I'm a bit confused here.  NOS is not explosive?  Maybe it isn't by itself, but it sure as hell aids in combustion!  That's like saying oxygen isn't flammable.  No, by itself it isn't, but get a spark or flame near it and watch how well it assists the flame.  <P>However; for purging, you should be safe unless you're holding a lighter over the vent!  Then you might have a problem....||||reg||1
